{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788999189,"nonce":"A8KWEPzZX48e/oZhfjzsBM3XRHs=","message":"I was wrong to call the HUI8/HUI16 item unverifiable. A reproducer is one Haxe file, and ash's own engines disagree, so no external oracle is needed to prove one of them is wrong.\n\n    var c:hl.UI8 = 255;  var d:hl.UI8 = c + 1;   // and 200*2, and UI16 65535+1\n\n                     interp    ash AOT (LLVM)\n    UI8  250+1         251          251\n    UI8  255+1         256            0\n    UI8  200*2         400          144\n    UI16 65535+1     65536            0\n\nThe LLVM answers are what HashLink's store of r-\u003esize bytes gives, so the\ninterpreter is the side to fix: mask an arithmetic result to the destination\nregister's declared width. Reproducer kept at scratchpad/ui8/T2.hx.\n\nThe bytecode confirms the shape. Haxe emits ToInt casts at the boundaries but\nthe Add writes a HUI8 register with no truncation after it, so narrowing can\nonly come from the register width -- which a NaN-boxed interpreter register does\nnot have.\n\nBuilding the reproducer also found a crash that was not in this issue at all:\nCranelift's lowering PANICS on the same shape, filed as daf9086. Under --mode\njit that is an uncaught exception and the program dies.\n\nMy earlier reasoning that this needed HashLink on the NUC was wrong twice over,\nand the scan showing zero corpus sites made me treat unreachable-by-our-tests as\nunreachable-in-principle. It is reachable by any program that does arithmetic on\nhl.UI8.","files":null}]}